.Dd November 15, 2026
.Dt ZFS-PRESAGE 8
.Os
.
.Sh NAME
.Nm zfs-presage
.Nd keep file or volume data resident in the ARC for a limited time
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm zfs
.Cm presage
.Op Fl V
.Op Fl s Ar start
.Op Fl t Ar ttl
.Op Fl o Ar offset
.Op Fl l Ar length
.Ar file Ns | Ns Ar volume Ns …
.Nm zfs
.Cm presage
.Fl t Cm 0
.Op Fl V
.Op Fl s Ar start
.Op Fl o Ar offset
.Op Fl l Ar length
.Ar file Ns | Ns Ar volume Ns …
.Nm zfs
.Cm presage status
.Op Fl p
.Op Fl V
.Op Fl o Ar offset
.Op Fl l Ar length
.Ar file Ns | Ns Ar volume Ns …
.
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Keeps the data of
.Ar file
or
.Ar volume
.Pq or the byte range given by Fl o No and Fl l
resident in the ARC from
.Ar start
for
.Ar ttl
seconds, regardless of what the adaptive replacement algorithm would do.
The request is a
.Em reservation :
it survives the command, may start in the future, and while active it
re-sweeps its range every few seconds so blocks written into the file
during the window become resident too.
That makes
.Dl : > backup.dump && zfs presage -t 2h backup.dump && pg_dump -Fc > backup.dump
a one-liner: the file is created empty, or emptied, first, because a
file must exist to be presaged and its old contents are not worth
loading; the reservation on the empty file then presages the dump's
blocks a few seconds after they are written out.
.Pp
Residency is bounded by
.Sy zfs_arc_presaged_max_percent
of the current ARC target
.Pq see Xr zfs 4 ,
which is 0 by default, i.e. the feature is off.
When the ARC target shrinks under memory pressure, the presaged data
above the new, smaller limit is demoted within about a second to the
most recently used end of the MFU list, and is then evicted like other
MFU data; the presaged data within the limit stays.
A request beyond the cap fails with
.Er ENOSPC
and whatever fitted stays resident.
.Bl -tag -width "-s start"
.It Fl s Ar start
When warming begins.
.Sy +duration
.Pq Sy +90s , +5m , +2h , +1d ,
.Sy HH:MM
.Pq today, or tomorrow if already past ,
.Sy YYYY-MM-DD Ns Oo T Ns HH:MM Ns Oo :SS Oc Oc
in local time, or seconds since the epoch.
Default: now.
With start in the future the command returns immediately; the status
subcommand reports when warming finished.
.It Fl t Ar ttl
How long residency lasts from
.Ar start ,
as seconds or
.Sy 90s , 5m , 2h , 1d .
Default: 30 minutes.
.Sy 0
kills: active residency on the file ends now and its pending reservations
are cancelled; with
.Fl s
only pending reservations starting at or after that time are cancelled.
A file or volume that has no reservation is left as it is.
To release presaged blocks that no reservation covers any more, presage
the file or volume again and then kill it, or set
.Sy zfs_arc_presaged_max_percent
to
.Sy 0 ,
which releases everything presaged.
.It Fl o Ar offset , Fl l Ar length
A byte range instead of the whole file or volume.
A later request on the same file and range replaces the earlier one.
.It Fl V
Every argument is the name of a volume
.Pq Sy tank/vm/disk0 .
Without
.Fl V ,
an argument that starts with
.Pa /dev/zvol/
is taken as a volume name, an argument that is a regular file is used as a
file, and anything else is tried as a volume name and, if there is no such
volume, opened as a file.
A directory or other file that is not a regular file is rejected.
A volume is presaged by name through the control node, so it need not be
open and needs no device node; that is the normal case when warming a
virtual machine's disk before the machine starts.
A snapshot or a filesystem name is rejected; give a filesystem a file path.
.It Cm status
Report, per file: bytes presaged, bytes otherwise cached, bytes not in
ARC, pending reservations, when warming finished and when the latest
reservation expires.
.Fl p
prints times as epoch seconds.
Status needs read permission on the file; everything else needs the
system configuration privilege
.Pq Sy CAP_SYS_ADMIN .
.El
.
.Sh NOTES
Requesting and killing residency require the same privilege as other
system configuration
.Pq Dv CAP_SYS_ADMIN No in the initial user namespace on Linux ,
for files and for volumes alike; the privilege cannot be delegated with
.Xr zfs-allow 8 .
.Cm status
needs only read access to the file, or, for a volume named on the command
line, that the volume is visible to the caller, as for
.Xr zfs-list 8 .
.Pp
The total amount presaged is bounded by the
.Sy zfs_arc_presaged_max_percent
module parameter, which is 0 (disabled) by default; see
.Xr zfs 4 .
A request that would exceed it presages what fits and fails with
.Er ENOSPC .
If memory pressure shrinks the ARC target, the presaged blocks above the
reduced limit are released early, as described above.
The limit bounds the presaged blocks themselves.
Reading presaged data with
.Xr read 2
or through a mapping makes copies in the dbuf cache, which are not counted
against it, so the memory held on behalf of presaged data can exceed the
limit by up to the size of the dbuf cache, 1/32 of the ARC by default.
An encrypted block that was written and then read is cached both as
stored and decrypted, and both forms count against the limit.
.Pp
Residency is per block, not per file.
While a reservation is active the file is swept again every few seconds
when its size or block count changed, so blocks appended during the
window are covered by that periodic re-sweep.
A block rewritten in place is covered on a best-effort basis: when the
rewrite completes before the deadline and the cap has room, the new copy
takes the old one's place in the presaged state at once
.Pq counted in the Sy presaged_inherited No arcstat ;
a whole-record write of a block no longer in the dbuf cache finds the old
copy by one ARC lookup before the write
.Pq Sy presaged_lookups , Sy presaged_inherited_lookup
and inherits the same way; a block demoted for the cap does not come back
by being rewritten.
A synchronous rewrite that reaches the disk ahead of its transaction
group
.Po
.Xr fsync 2
or
.Dv O_SYNC
on a dataset with
.Sy logbias Ns = Ns Sy throughput ,
large synchronous writes with any
.Sy logbias
.Pc
inherits when that transaction group is synced, a few seconds later;
until then the new copy is cached but not presaged.
A write with
.Dv O_DIRECT
leaves no copy in the ARC and does not inherit: such a block is covered
again only by the periodic re-sweep, when the file's size or allocation
changed, and the copy it replaces leaves the presaged state when the
write is synced out.
Keeping a heavily rewritten file fully resident is therefore not
guaranteed; the reservation is best effort for changing data.
A snapshot does not disturb a presaged file, and rewriting the file under
a snapshot does not grow what is presaged: the deadline moves from the
overwritten copy to the new one.
Blocks that a truncation, a hole punch or the deletion of the file removes
from it leave the presaged state when the change is written out, whether
or not a snapshot keeps them.
The metadata block that holds the file's dnode, which the file shares with
its neighbours, is not among them: after a deletion it stays until the
deadline, 16 KiB per file at most.
A block shared by several files, through a clone, a reflink copy or
deduplication, is presaged for all of them, and where more than one of
them is presaged it stays until the latest of their deadlines.
It leaves early when any of the files that share it frees or rewrites it,
or when the reservation of one of them is killed; presaging the remaining
file again restores it.
A request made while the dataset's
.Sy primarycache
is other than
.Sy all
does not presage its data and reports it as it finds it, cached or not.
Data presaged before the property was changed stays until its deadline.
Reads with
.Dv O_DIRECT
do not use presaged data.
An application that keeps its own cache and both reads and writes with
.Dv O_DIRECT
gains nothing from a reservation on its files and pays for the re-sweep,
which reads back from disk what the application wrote.
.Pp
A second request for the same file and range replaces the first, even
with a shorter TTL.
If the second request starts later, what the first had presaged is
released at once and loaded again at the new start.
Where reservations for different ranges overlap, a block stays until
the latest of their deadlines.
.Pp
Reservations are kept in memory only and are forgotten on export, reboot
or module unload.
Unmounting a filesystem releases its presaged files before the unmount
completes, and
.Nm zfs Cm unload-key
releases the presaged volumes that use the key, so that nothing decrypted
is held in memory once a key is unloaded.
An
.Cm unload-key
that fails has still ended the reservations of the volumes under the
dataset and released their blocks.
While a key is being unloaded, the presaged volumes of the whole pool
are left alone: a request for one of them loads nothing until the unload
has ended, and a release waits likewise.
A request made meanwhile for a volume that uses the key reports 0 bytes
and is dropped, as the volume can no longer be read.
A filesystem that is unmounted and mounted again, which
.Nm zfs Cm rename
and a change of
.Sy mountpoint
also do, therefore starts with nothing presaged.
A restore releases every presaged file and volume on the dataset it
restores:
.Xr zfs-rollback 8 ,
a rollback made by a channel program, and any
.Xr zfs-receive 8
into an existing dataset, incremental or
.Fl F .
What was asked for on the discarded state is not presumed wanted on the
restored one, so the reservations are cancelled, the blocks are released
within seconds without any file data being read, and the request can be
made again in a quieter moment after the restore.
Blocks the restore left unreferenced leave as the asynchronous destroy
frees them.
A restore waits only for the step a load in progress is on, at most ten
seconds: a
.Nm zfs Cm presage
that is still loading the file or volume stops within one 64 MiB step,
reports what it had loaded, and its reservation is cancelled with the
rest.
A request made while a restore is in progress loads nothing and reports
0 bytes; it is cancelled with the rest if the restore replaces the
dataset's contents, and is loaded once the restore has ended if it does
not.
A release asked for meanwhile, with
.Fl t Ar 0
or by a request that starts later, takes effect once the restore has
ended.
The
.Sy zfs/presage
kstat reports how many reservations exist, how many are pending or
active, how many loads are in flight and how many dataset holds presage
holds at the moment.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr zfs 4 ,
.Xr zfsprops 7
